* the main result is
	* certain type of line-integrals have extremum where the Euler equation is true
		\ id wdxehzwqtplkmncw
	* proposition
		* if
			* n : Nat+
				* the number of dimensions of the space
			* ts, te : Real
				* ts < te
			* I := [ts .. te]
			* Line := (I -> Real ^ n, having continuous slope)
			* f : (Real, Real ^ n, Real ^ n) -> Real
				* the function integrated over l
				* has continuous slope
			* eval f l t := f (t, l t, slope l t)
			* A : Line -> Real := eval f >>> integral (over I)
			* slope-k - the partial slope of a function in its (k+1)-th input
		* then
			* l is extremum argument of A
			* <=>
			* eval (slope-1 f) l = slope (eval (slope-2 f) l)
